// Lesson 5.3 — Into the Cave: Scenes, Persistence & The Refactor
// The architectural payoff lesson. The student walks into a cave (the switch has
// worked since they gave the Player a collider in 2.1), discovers that every
// number they own comes back reset, and refactors coins, health, maxHealth and
// the axe flag off PlayerController onto the StatManager singleton that has been
// an empty stub since Unit 2.
//
// Scaffolding: FULL WALKTHROUGH (level 1) — and TWO of them, because this
// lesson introduces two mechanisms with no precedent anywhere in the course.
// A SINGLETON (a static slot, an Awake that claims it, DontDestroyOnLoad, and
// a guard against the second copy) and an ON-SCREEN HUD (a Canvas, a TMP text
// object, and a script that reads the numbers from the manager instead of the
// player). The ladder is per-CONCEPT, not per-week, so both are level 1 and
// neither withholds a line. verify-site reads the level from EVERY walkthrough
// on the page, refuses a page whose walkthroughs disagree, and sums the gaps
// across all of them — so a second walkthrough that quietly withheld lines
// fails the build. The DECOMPOSE block is level 1 too: every part named,
// explained and sourced, because "keep my stats across a scene" has never been
// built here either.
//
// STRUCTURE (this pass, the editor/code split): everything that happens in a
// Unity pane is an explicit "In the Editor" numbered list — playing the run that
// loses everything, reading the build's scene list, reading the `int` on a cave
// mouth, opening the empty StatManager stub, and the whole Canvas / Text (TMP) /
// anchor / HudController / slot-wiring job. The file work is "In the Code": the
// five-row API table, the refactor notes, one Socratic block, then the two
// walkthroughs. WALK_HUD's entire first step was Inspector work in a code
// container (the defect this pass exists to kill) and is now editor steps 6-8.
//
// CULLED FOR REPETITION (with the survivor named):
//   - The "This is not a bug" trap callout, which told the story that Break It
//     Down parts 1 and 2 already tell — the scene load destroys and rebuilds,
//     and SceneSwitch.cs handles the things that SHOULD reset. The SURVIVOR is
//     the decompose pair, because the split between "this room" and "this run"
//     is the lesson's spine and the callout was a second telling of it. The
//     observation it framed survives as editor step 1, where the student
//     actually watches it happen.
//   - The four-column scene table and the topology paragraph under it: between
//     them they restated the decompose's part 1 and part 2, and every fact a
//     student needs from them is now a pane step (the build's scene list and the
//     `int` on a cave mouth) — four columns of lookup compressed into the two
//     places the numbers can be read.
//   - The "You have already seen persistence done the wrong way" callout: the
//     same `static`-field point is the first Socratic question and the doc
//     pointer of decompose part 2. Third telling, and it went.
//   - The "Build Lab: The Refactor" opening paragraph, which repeated the
//     walkthrough's own intro sentence for sentence (an empty stub since Unit 2,
//     a reason that arrives from a scene change), and the console-vs-screen
//     paragraph before the HUD: the HUD walkthrough's own `Debug.Log` line makes
//     the same point where the student meets the code.
//   - The Canvas trap callout (90 words) folded into the cost cell of the road
//     it is actually about: it only ever applied if you drag the Overworld's
//     Canvas across, and it now sits in that row instead of floating below both.
//   - The API table's `Object.FindFirstObjectByType<T>()` row and its
//     `TextMeshProUGUI.text` row: the first is 5.2's own table row, and the
//     second is annotated twice in WALK_HUD where the student types it — the
//     `using TMPro;` line and the `.text` assignment both explain it. Six rows
//     became five.
//   - The trailing paragraph's second half ("break one thing at a time and bring
//     the old bug back on purpose"), which promised a fault hunt the page does
//     not contain. The verification walk survives, as the editor half's last
//     step.
//
