<p class="lesson-lead">
    You break the door, you walk in — and everything you own breaks with it. Your coins are gone,
    your health is back to full, your axe has vanished. Nothing is wrong with your code: you left
    one scene and entered another. Today you meet the biggest refactor of the year.
</p>

${renderAskBanner('how do I keep my health when I change scenes?', 1)}

${renderDecompose('5.3', DECOMPOSE, 1)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">In the Editor</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~30 min</span>
    </div>

    <ol>
        <li>
            <strong>Game view →</strong> press Play in the <strong>Overworld</strong> and play for a
            minute: collect a few coins, take a spike hit, pick up the axe. Then walk into one of the
            cave mouths. <em>Everything you were carrying is gone.</em> Notice what did follow you
            across — your position, your facing, your animation controller — and what did not.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>File → Build Profiles → Scene List</strong> (older tutorials call this Build
            Settings): three scenes, and their order <em>is</em> the load index —
            <strong>Start (index 0)</strong>, Overworld (index 1), Platformer (index 2).
            <strong>Start (index 0)</strong> is what a built game loads first, and it is the scene
            that contains the StatManager object — and no Canvas at all.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Hierarchy → Overworld → one of the two cave mouths → Inspector → Scene Switch →
            Scene</strong>: an <code>int</code>, holding <code>0</code> on one mouth and
            <code>2</code> on the other. That number is a <em>position in that list</em>, not a
