    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Three ways to make a number survive</h3>
        <p>All three are real Unity techniques. Compare what each one costs you:</p>
        <table>
            <tr><th>Approach</th><th>How it works</th><th>What it costs</th></tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>A <code>static</code> field</strong></td>
                <td>Belongs to the <em>class</em> — destroying the objects does not touch it.</td>
                <td>No lifecycle: you cannot reset it cleanly, and in the editor it survives between Play sessions, so testing lies to you.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>A persistent singleton object</strong></td>
                <td>One object registers as <em>the</em> instance and asks Unity not to destroy it.</td>
                <td>You must handle the <em>second</em> copy a scene load brings.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>A ScriptableObject asset</strong></td>
                <td>Lives in a project asset rather than in a scene, so no scene load can touch it.</td>
                <td>A new concept and one more asset to manage — and it survives Play sessions too, which is either the point or a nuisance.</td>
            </tr>
        </table>
        <ol>
<li>Your stats should reset when you start a new game, and survive a scene change. Which of the three can express both of those, and which one cannot express the first?</li>
<li>Start (index 0) already contains its own StatManager object. If you walk back to it later, what will be there — and what happens if nothing stops it from registering itself a second time?</li>
<li>Why can position <em>not</em> be handled this way? What is different about position that makes <code>SceneSwitch.cs</code> handle it instead?</li>
</ol>
